---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { offer, otherPlants, plantCount, type PlantGroup } from "../data/offer";
import { chrysanthemumStrip, pansyStrip, type GalleryPhoto } from "../data/gallery";
import { currentSeason } from "../data/season";

/**
 * The offer on the home page: one card per category page, each opening with two of that
 * group's own photographs.
 *
 * This is what replaced the flat list of fourteen plants that used to sit here. The home
 * page's job is to say what the holding sells and send people to the right page; the
 * descriptions are the plant names themselves, taken from the collection, so nothing here
 * is copy that has to be kept in step with the offer by hand.
 *
 * The four pages come from `src/data/offer.ts` and no longer from a list in this file - the
 * same four addresses used to be written out both here and in `navigation.ts`.
 *
 * ## The season chip, which this block spent two releases without
 *
 * Every tile carries one, in three states: "W sprzedaży", "Wkrótce", "Poza sezonem". That is
 * a deliberate reversal of what stood here, and the argument it reverses was not a bad one,
 * so it is kept rather than deleted: the marker lived on these tiles for one release and was
 * taken off because the year is `SeasonCards`' subject, not this block's, and because four
 * equal doors to four pages is what this block is for - a door that changes colour with the
 * date being a worse door.
 *
 * The owner asked for it back in September 2026, having seen both. The reasoning that lost:
 * someone arriving here in July and clicking "Chryzantemy" meets a full catalogue with no
 * hint that nothing in it is for sale for another three months, and the season strip a screen
 * above does not travel with them.
 *
 * **The cost is real and is worth watching.** Three of the four groups are out of season for
 * most of the year, so most of the time this section shows three greyed doors and one lit
 * one. If that turns out to tell too gloomy a story, the cheap fix is not to remove the chip
 * but to print it only where there is something to say - `currentSeason.groups[group]` is
 * already `"now" | "soon" | null`, so it is one condition in the template below.
 *
 * Recorded in docs/inwentaryzacja.md with both halves of the argument.
 *
 * ## The mosaic: two frames, always
 *
 * **The count is fixed at two, and that is the whole design.** It was "up to three, whatever
 * the collection happens to hold", and every problem this block has had came from that
 * number moving: with the ratio on each frame, the pansy tile - one plant, one photograph -
 * had a strip three times taller than the tile holding eleven, so the smallest group was the
 * heaviest thing in the section. Moving the ratio onto the strip levelled the heights and
 * broke the frames instead: three came out nearly square, two came out as 110x57 letterboxes
 * on `Rabatowe i wieloletnie`.
 *
 * Two frames in two fixed columns has neither failure, because there is no longer a variable
 * to fail on. Every tile is the same height and every frame is the same shape.
 *
 * **The frames are 4:5, not the 4:3 they were until September 2026.** That is a decision
 * about the sources, not about the look: six of the eight photographs these tiles can draw on
 * are portrait, and a 4:3 frame was throwing away 47% of them. At 4:5 it keeps 89%, and the
 * strip goes from 225x82 to 225x136 at 1200px - from two postage stamps to something that
 * reads as the product this business sells. The two landscape sources pay for it, keeping 45%
 * and 53%. The ratio is written in two places that must agree; the CSS says which and why.
 *
 * **`PlantEntry` reached the same diagnosis and a different answer, and the difference is
 * deliberate.** It now gives each photograph its own ratio, capped at 3:4, so nothing is
 * cropped unless it is taller than that. These tiles cannot do the same: four of them stand in
 * one row and the whole history above is about what happens when the frame shape is allowed to
 * vary - it was tried twice and failed twice. An entry is one photograph on its own; a tile is
 * one of four that have to match. Do not harmonise them.
 *
 * Two is also the number the content can actually guarantee. `Rabatowe` has exactly two
 * entries with a photograph and no `PhotoStrip` to borrow from, so it can never reach three;
 * a design needing three would be permanently broken on one of the four tiles.
 *
 * **The name list is capped at four.** It is still generated from the collection and still
 * cannot drift from the offer, but it no longer prints all of it: eleven balcony plants ran to
 * five lines while the pansy tile ran to one, and because the tiles share a height that
 * difference came out as a hole in three tiles out of four. Four names and a tail - `i 7
 * innych` - say what the wall of eleven was saying badly. The full list is one click away, on
 * the page the tile is a door to.
 *
 * Which two: **the collection chooses, not a list here.** The group's entries in `order` that
 * have an `image`, then, if that is fewer than two, the group's own `PhotoStrip` - the same
 * frames that sit under the list on its page. The day dahlia, pelargonia bluszczolistna or
 * sundaville gets a photograph it joins the queue by itself.
 *
 * The frames are decorative - `alt=""` - and that is deliberate rather than lazy. The whole
 * card is one link (see below); its accessible name is the text inside it, which already
 * names every plant in the mosaic. Repeating `imageAlt` here would make a screen reader read
 * two long sentences before the name of the group they belong to.
 */
const plants = (await getCollection("plants")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

/** Where a group short of two frames borrows the rest. Deliberately here and not in
 *  `offer.ts`: these are the same photographs the category pages show under their lists, so
 *  this is a presentation choice, and keeping it here spares every page that imports the
 *  menu from pulling the image imports in with it. Only `Bratki` needs it today - one entry,
 *  four strip frames. */
const stripFallback: Partial<Record<PlantGroup, GalleryPhoto[]>> = {
  Bratki: pansyStrip,
  Chryzantemy: chrysanthemumStrip,
};

const tiles = offer
  .map((page) => {
    const members = plants.filter((plant) => page.groups.includes(plant.data.group));
    const fromEntries = members
      .filter((plant) => plant.data.image !== undefined)
      .map((plant) => plant.data.image!);
    const fromStrips = page.groups
      .flatMap((group) => stripFallback[group] ?? [])
      .map((photo) => photo.src);

    /* A page can cover more than one group (none does today, but the type allows it), so the
       chip takes the most advanced state any of its groups is in: selling beats coming up
       beats out of season. A tile that said "Poza sezonem" while one of its groups was on
       sale would be plainly wrong. */
    const states = page.groups.map((group) => currentSeason.groups[group]);
    const state = states.includes("now") ? "now" : states.includes("soon") ? "soon" : null;

    /* The names are the tile description and they come from the collection, so they can never
       drift from the offer. What they could not do was stay the same length: eleven balcony
       plants ran to five lines while the pansy tile ran to one, and since the tiles are the
       same height that difference came out as a hole in three tiles out of four. Four names
       and a tail say "there are more of these", which is what the wall of eleven was saying
       badly. The full list is on the category page, one click away. */
    const allNames = members.map((plant) => plant.data.name);
    const shownNames = allNames.slice(0, 4);

    return {
      ...page,
      names: allNames,
      shownNames,
      rest: allNames.length - shownNames.length,
      photos: [...fromEntries, ...fromStrips].slice(0, 2),
      state,
      stateLabel: state === "now" ? "W sprzedaży" : state === "soon" ? "Wkrótce" : "Poza sezonem",
    };
  })
  // A card for an empty group would lead to a page with a heading and no list.
  .filter((page) => page.names.length > 0);
---

<section class="overview" aria-labelledby="oferta-title">
  <div class="overview__head">
    <h2 class="overview__title" id="oferta-title">Kwiaty w naszej ofercie</h2>
    <p class="overview__lead">
      Kwiaty z naszej uprawy. Przy każdej roślinie są najważniejsze informacje o jej uprawie i o
      kolorach dostępnych u nas.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="overview__grid">
    {
      tiles.map((tile) => (
        <li>
          <a class="tile" href={tile.href}>
            {tile.photos.length > 0 && (
              <span class:list={["tile__photos", tile.photos.length === 1 && "tile__photos--one"]}>
                {tile.photos.map((photo) => (
                  <Picture
                    class="tile__photo"
                    src={photo}
                    formats={["avif", "webp"]}
                    fallbackFormat="jpg"
                    widths={[240, 480]}
                    sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 110px, (min-width: 700px) 16vw, 45vw"
                    width={240}
                    height={300}
                    alt=""
                    loading="lazy"
                  />
                ))}
              </span>
            )}
            <h3 class="tile__title">{tile.tileTitle}</h3>
            <p class="tile__names">
              {tile.shownNames.join(" · ")}
              {tile.rest > 0 && <span class="tile__rest"> {otherPlants(tile.rest)}</span>}
            </p>
            <div class="tile__foot">
              <p class="tile__more">{plantCount(tile.names.length)}</p>
              <span class:list={["tile__tag", tile.state && "tile__tag--lit"]}>
                {tile.stateLabel}
              </span>
            </div>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</section>

<style>
  .overview {
    background: var(--paper-clay);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: var(--pad-section) var(--edge);
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .overview__head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem 1.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .overview__title {
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
  }

  .overview__lead {
    max-width: 42ch;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .overview__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.4rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 260px), 1fr));
  }

  /* The whole card is the link, so the target is the card and not a word inside it. */
  /* Flex column rather than grid, for one reason: `margin-top: auto` on the foot. The four
     tiles hold two, three and four lines of plant names, and with the rows packed to the top
     the counter and the season chip sat at four different heights across one row of cards.
     A repeated element belongs in the same place on every copy of the card. */
  .tile {
    height: 100%;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    /* --rule-dim, not --rule, and this is the one border on the site that needs the darker one.
       Every other 1px line here sits on --paper and gets 1.27:1 out of --rule. This one has
       --paper-clay on its outside, where --rule falls to 1.18:1, while the tile fill lifts off
       that ground by only 1.079:1 - a faint plate inside a faint line, and the edge went soft
       from both directions at once. --rule-dim reads 1.35:1 against the fill and 1.25:1 against
       the ground: still a hairline, but one with an edge. */
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-dim);
    /* --paper, not --paper-dim. The ground under these tiles is --paper-clay, and a dimmed
       tile on clay is 1.003:1 - the fill would be drawn and seen by nobody. --paper gives
       1.08:1, which is the faint but real lift every card on this site uses. */
    background: var(--paper);
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .tile:hover {
    border-color: var(--green);
    background: var(--paper);
  }

  /* Two columns, fixed - see the note at the top of this file. Both the ratio and the count
     are constants here, which is why the tiles cannot drift out of alignment with each
     other: every strip is the same height because every tile is the same width. */
  .tile__photos {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }

  /* A group that cannot muster two frames spans its one across both columns rather than
     leaving a hole, and takes the ratio that keeps the strip the height it would have had.
     No group is in that state today; a new one would be, on the day it gets its first
     photograph and before it gets its second. */
  .tile__photos--one {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  .tile__photos--one .tile__photo {
    aspect-ratio: 8 / 5;
  }

  /* 4:5, not the 4:3 these frames had until September 2026, and the reason is in the sources
     rather than in taste: six of the eight photographs these tiles draw on are portrait, and a
     4:3 frame was keeping 53% of them. At 4:5 it keeps 89%, and the strip grows from 82px tall
     to 137px at 1200px - from two postage stamps to something that reads as the product. The
     two landscape sources (pelargonia rabatowa, chryzantema wielkokwiatowa) pay for it: they
     keep 45% and 53%. Every crop here is centred and none of them is aimed.

     **The ratio is written twice and the two must move together** - here, and as width/height
     on the <Picture> above. That pair is what makes Astro emit a 4:5 file; if CSS asks for one
     ratio while Astro was told another, the photograph is cropped twice and the second crop
     cannot be aimed. */
  .tile__photo {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .tile:hover .tile__photo {
    border-color: var(--green);
  }
  /* The whole door answers, not only the frames inside it. */
  .tile:hover {
    border-color: var(--green);
  }

  /* Counter and chip share the foot of the tile. They were first drawn beside the title, which
     is where the mockup put them - but four tiles across 1200px leaves each one about 180px
     wide, and an 85px chip on that row squeezed "Rabatowe i wieloletnie" onto three lines. The
     two are the same kind of thing anyway: small uppercase metadata about the group, as
     against the title and the plant names, which are the group. */
  .tile__foot {
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 0.15rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.4rem 0.75rem;
  }

  /* Lit or not, with the three labels doing the rest - the same arrangement as the season
     cards, and it works here for the same reason: the calendar marks at most one window at a
     time, so at most one tile is ever lit and there is nothing for a second fill to mean. */
  .tile__tag {
    margin-inline-start: auto;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--ink-grey);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-dim);
    padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem;
  }

  .tile__tag--lit {
    color: var(--paper);
    background: var(--green-deep);
    border-color: var(--green-deep);
  }

  .tile__title {
    text-wrap: balance;
    font-size: var(--text-h3);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .tile__names {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* The tail is not one of the names, so it does not read as one: same size, grey rather than
     soft ink, and it always closes the list. */
  .tile__rest {
    color: var(--ink-grey);
  }

  /* A counter, and counters are the accent's other half - see .eyebrow in global.css. */
  .tile__more {
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ochre);
  }
  /* Hover goes to ink rather than to green: the resting state is already the accent, and
     swapping one colour for another reads as a state change nobody asked for. */
  .tile:hover .tile__more {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
